2527 0 obj
<<
  /Title <>
  /Subject (Video game research often treats games as a monolithic category, and single-game or single-platform studies leave open whether findings generalise. This Registered Report used Open Play digital trace data to test whether (H1) total playtime is meaningfully associated with mental wellbeing and (H2) this association differs by game genre. The data comprise 222,000 hours of playtime across 4,128 games from Nintendo, Xbox, and Steam for 1,668 UK and US adults, linked to genre metadata and 6,518 biweekly wellbeing assessments (SWEMWBS). Judged against a preregistered threshold for practical significance, neither within-person fluctuations nor habitual differences in total playtime were meaningfully linked to wellbeing, supporting H1. With each game filed under its first-listed genre, as preregistered, the joint tests detected no genre differences, so H2 was not supported. Departing from the preregistration because most games carry several genre labels, we also counted each game towards all its genres; the tests then detected within-person but not between-person differences, a within-person result that did not survive imputation of missing waves. An exploratory hierarchical model placed every genre's within-person association close to zero, with fewer than one of the 14 genres expected to lie beyond the threshold from the average genre effect; a model of genre composition estimated this spread less precisely. An exploratory daily-timescale analysis gave the same within-person picture for total playtime and the hierarchical model's genre spread. Genre labels appear too coarse a proxy for the psychological experiences that plausibly drive gaming's wellbeing effects; finer-grained characterisations of play are more promising.)
  /Keywords (video games, wellbeing, genres, digital trace data, Registered Report)
  /Author <>
  /Creator (Typst 0.14.2)
  /ModDate (D:20261028144202Z)
  /CreationDate (D:20261028144202Z)
>>
endobj

